# What this proves, and what it does not:
#
# [V] the descriptor advertises three bit depths: the harness reads
#     kOfxImageEffectPropSupportedBitDepths and the run gets RGBA16F and RGBA8
#     images back without a refusal (the plug-in's render() accepts a depth only
#     if a pixel size exists for it; eUShort is still refused).
# [V] the CPU path renders both depths with the same rule as its kernel: the
#     half loop converts through float with one round-to-nearest-even step, the
#     byte loop multiplies the stored code value, rounds and saturates. The
#     harness compares the produced frame against source*gain quantised to the
#     same depth, bit for bit, over a frame of 4096-step fractions (half) and
#     0..255 code values (byte), at 64x48, 162 checks per host profile.
# [V] the depth is taken from the image, not from a parameter: the same run
#     renders RGBA32F, RGBA16F and RGBA8 frames without re-creating the instance.
# [U] the two new launchers (LaunchGainRGBA16F, LaunchGainRGBA8) were never
#     compiled: this cell has no nvcc, so nothing in Kernel.cu was built or run.
#     Their kernels are covered - 162 half shapes and 164 byte cases through
#     test/shim, evidence byte-and-half-kernels.log - and the launchers add
#     device selection, the grid and the alignment refusal, which only a CUDA
#     build can compile.
# [U] every GPU number at either depth: no device ran this.
